WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.600
In the made-up budget, decomposers respired far more than the grazers and predators.

2
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:13.560
Most plant production was never eaten by a grazer at all.

3
00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:17.283
Was that just a quirk of invented numbers?

4
00:00:18.503 --> 00:00:23.143
In a real ecosystem, where does the plants' production actually end up each year?

5
00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:26.607
John Teal asked this on the coast of Georgia.

6
00:00:27.807 --> 00:00:32.427
He built an energy budget for a salt marsh, and published it in 1962.

7
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:40.460
One grass dominates these marshes, smooth cordgrass It grows in the tidal mud and

8
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.640
makes a great deal of plant matter each year.

9
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:47.830
A grazing-chain picture of ecology predicts the obvious route.

10
00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:52.630
Grass feeds the herbivores, and then the herbivores feed the predators.

11
00:00:55.233 --> 00:00:56.833
Teal did not assume the route.

12
00:00:58.013 --> 00:01:02.093
He assembled the budget from measurements of the grass and the algae on the mud.

13
00:01:03.313 --> 00:01:07.413
It also drew on measurements of the animals and bacteria living there.

14
00:01:09.717 --> 00:01:12.997
Insects and other animals graze on the living grass.

15
00:01:14.137 --> 00:01:17.357
Will they take most of its production, or only a small part?

16
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:24.870
Only a small part.

17
00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:29.190
In Teal's budget, the purple pipe to the grazers was thin.

18
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:32.430
The grazing chain was a side channel.

19
00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:36.920
Much of the grass died instead, and broke into fragments.

20
00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:42.780
Bacteria used those fragments, and so did animals that feed on detritus.

21
00:01:43.833 --> 00:01:46.453
Those detritus feeders are themselves eaten.

22
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:50.373
So detritus is not a dead end for animals.

23
00:01:51.593 --> 00:01:53.653
It is a second route into the food web.

24
00:01:54.717 --> 00:01:57.317
Every organism on this route respires.

25
00:01:58.617 --> 00:02:00.737
Each one releases heat through a grey door.

26
00:02:02.017 --> 00:02:04.197
The energy never returns to the grass.

27
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:06.820
Only matter cycles.

28
00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:10.380
Nutrients return to the grass through its roots.

29
00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:15.440
Carbon returns as carbon dioxide, which the leaves fix again.

30
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:18.440
The energy comes from fresh sunlight.

31
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.800
Teal's budget also left a remainder.

32
00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:28.460
After the respiration of bacteria and animals, about 45% percent of net production

33
00:02:28.460 --> 00:02:29.460
was left over.

34
00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:33.683
He took it as available to leave the marsh with the tides.

35
00:02:34.923 --> 00:02:37.403
But it was a remainder, not a measured export.

36
00:02:38.483 --> 00:02:40.923
Even so, the study changed the picture.

37
00:02:42.223 --> 00:02:47.723
With others' work on decomposing marsh grass, it moved detritus from a footnote to

38
00:02:47.723 --> 00:02:49.143
the centre of coastal budgets.

39
00:02:51.533 --> 00:02:53.833
So keep the brown door in every budget.

40
00:02:55.133 --> 00:03:00.133
In this marsh, most grass production went that way rather than to grazers, and

41
00:03:00.133 --> 00:03:02.513
respiration released its energy as heat.
